"use strict";(self.webpackChunklost_notch=self.webpackChunklost_notch||[]).push([["607"],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"church-pond","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/church-pond","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-10-19-church-pond/index.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-19-church-pond/index.mdx","title":"Church Pond","description":"Church Pond is a little more than a mile off the Kangamagus Highway, but it feels much more remote. The usual route to the pond is from Camp Passaconaway via the Church Pond Loop Trail. While this used to be a loop, the east section of the trail is permanently closed, now making it an out-and-back route.","date":"2026-10-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Carrigain Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/carrigain-region","description":"The region around Mt. Carrigain and the Moats"}],"readingTime":1,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"church-pond","title":"Church Pond","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","carrigain-region"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Monadnock after a Nor\'easter","permalink":"/blog/monadnock-under-snow"}},"content":"Church Pond is a little more than a mile off the Kangamagus Highway, but it feels much more remote. The usual route to the pond is from Camp Passaconaway via the Church Pond Loop Trail. While this used to be a loop, the east section of the trail is permanently closed, now making it an out-and-back route.\\n\\nAfter walking over bog bridges through a beautiful marsh, you arrive at a knoll on the east side of the pond. Follow one of the herd paths down to the shore for some great views especially to the southwest. Here is a dramatic view of Mt. Potash (left) rising on the other side of the Kangamagus. To the right of Mt. Potash is the valley of Sabbaday Brook with the Sleepers in the distance.\\n\\n![Mt. Potash](./potash.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nSwing to the right just a little and North Tripyramid comes into view.\\n\\n![North Tripyramid](./tripyramid.jpg)\\n\\nAlthough it\'s definitely past peak foliage, the tamaracks are almost neon yellow this time of year. \\n\\n![Tamaracks](./tamaracks.jpg)\\n\\nTamaracks are common in New Hampshire, but they almost never dominate the landscape. It\'s always a pleasant surprise to catch the tamarack show after most hardwoods have dropped their leaves."},{"id":"monadnock-under-snow","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/monadnock-under-snow","editUrl":"https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2023-03-16-monadnock-under-snow/index.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2023-03-16-monadnock-under-snow/index.mdx","title":"Monadnock after a Nor\'easter","description":"Yesterday, a late season nor\'easter dumped more than two feet of snow on the Monadnock region. The entire summit of Monadnock is under a blanket of white. If you look closely, you can see a lone hiker near the top of the mountain.","date":"2023-03-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Mt. Monadnock","permalink":"/blog/tags/monadnock","description":"Mt. Monadnock"}],"readingTime":0.48,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"monadnock-under-snow","title":"Monadnock after a Nor\'easter","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["monadnock"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Church Pond","permalink":"/blog/church-pond"}},"content":"Yesterday, a late season nor\'easter dumped more than two feet of snow on the Monadnock region. The entire summit of Monadnock is under a blanket of white. If you look closely, you can see a lone hiker near the top of the mountain.\\n\\n<div class=\\"text--center\\">\\n\\n![Monadnock summit](./summit.jpg)\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThe high winds sculpted dramatic shapes in the snow.\\n\\n![Wind sculpted snow](./sculpted-snow.jpg)\\n\\nThe underlying rock emerges in places to remind us the snow is temporary. Spring is just around the corner. The melting snow will help the alpine plants thrive. \\n\\n![Detail](./detail.jpg)"}]}}')}}]);